\section{\emph{Morsels} for Very Large Superpages}

Traditional monolithic operating systems allocate static arrays of page
descriptors proportional to total physical RAM, resulting in
significant memory overhead on high-capacity server systems. Telix
addresses this by adapting the \emph{Morsel-Driven} allocation paradigm
originally developed for high-throughput database query engines
\footcite{kemp14}.

Physical memory is partitioned into elastic, self-contained memory
blocks ("morsels"). Instead of linear page descriptor lookup tables,
translation mapping overhead is decoupled from address space size
through software-managed inverted and clustered page tables
\footcite{tall95, huck93}:

\begin{equation}
T_{\texttt{lookup}}(\texttt{VA}) = H(\texttt{VPN}) \pmod{N_{\texttt{buckets}}}
\end{equation}

Where $H(\texttt{VPN})$ is a hashed virtual page number mapping to a
fixed-size bucket array, ensuring sublinear metadata bounds $O(M)$ where
$M$ is the active working set rather than total physical capacity $P$.
\\
Rik van Riel posted something akin to morsels to the Linux Kernel
Mailing List. The idea was that 1 GiB or larger superpages would be
treated as arenas for large-scale memory management with buddy bitmaps
attached etc. and decisions like keeping them fully intact or only
touched with movable memory could be made at that level.

\section{Superpaging and Page Clustering}

To minimize Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) misses, Telix uses
transparent superpage allocation \footcite{nava02} backed by shared page
table hierarchies across co-located threads \footcite{ko03}. Physical
allocation utilizes page clustering algorithms derived from BSD VM
extensions \footcite{baba79} and Linux kernel VM optimizations
\footcite{dick01} to preserve contiguous physical memory pools.
