\section{Effective Use of Inverted Pagetables}

Just using a real API instead of a data structure crossdressing every
architecture as x86 enables the effective use of inverted pagetables,
which radically conserve space relative to naïve schemes like unshared
radix tree pagetables.

\section{Shared Pagetables}

Where radix tree pagetables are demanded by hardware, or where other
multiway trees are used, sharing nodes of the tree structures is needed
to mitigate data structure proliferation. Naïvely doing this can cause
contention, hence per-CPU or per-NUMA-node replication should also be
part of such a strategy to prevent that. This also still applies with
lockfree or fine-grained-locked pagetables because the atomic updates
can still contend for too broadly shared of cachelines.

