\section{Page Clustering, \texttt{Svnapot}, and Sub-Page Granularity}
\label{sec:page_clustering_napot}

Modern workload memory access patterns require flexible page granularity to minimize TLB pressure while avoiding internal fragmentation. Telix abstracts underlying MMU capabilities into three primary allocation tiers:

\subsection{Naturally Aligned Power-of-Two (\texttt{Svnapot}) Allocation}
On RISC-V platforms supporting \texttt{Svnapot}, the memory allocator groups contiguous 4~KiB page frames into 64~KiB clusters. By setting the $N$ bit (bit 63) in the leaf PTE, the hardware MMU collapses 16 physical pages into a single TLB translation entry, reducing TLB miss latency by up to 16$\times$ for bulk data buffers. The \texttt{Svnapot} extension also allows the showcasing of how {page clustering}\index{page clustering} interoperates with superpaging\index{superpaging} for dense page size spectra.

\subsection{Sub-4~KiB Memory Domains via MIPS PageGrain}
On micro-controller nodes, Telix reconfigures the page table walker via the MIPS \texttt{PageGrain} interface to manage memory at 1~KiB granularity. This allows fine-grained memory protection for lightweight driver routines and embedded continuations without reserving entire 4~KiB frames. The 1~KiB granularity also allows the showcasing of the power of {page clustering}\index{page clustering}.

\subsection{Capability-Backed Memory Allocation}
When executing on CHERI hardware (such as Arm Morello or CHERI-RISC-V), page tables manage hardware capability bounds rather than coarse page permissions. Memory allocations yield typed, dynamically bounded capabilities that prevent buffer overflows and use-after-free vulnerabilities directly within the Framekernel address space.

\chapter{Memory Architecture: Sublinear Metadata and Granularity}
\label{ch:memory}

Modern monolithic operating systems suffer from severe metadata bloat. As physical memory capacities scale into the terabytes, the traditional \texttt{mem\_map} array (which allocates a static descriptor for every physical hardware page) consumes gigabytes of RAM purely for bookkeeping. Furthermore, the conflation of physical allocation units with virtual mapping granularities leads to brittle architectures that struggle to balance ABI compatibility with TLB efficiency.

Telix completely redesigns memory management around three principles: **ABI-Preserving Page Clustering** (reducing physical tracking overhead), **Coremaplessness** (sublinear physical metadata via lock-free, in-band allocators), and **Extent-Based Virtual Indexing** (sublinear virtual metadata via software inverted page tables).

\section{ABI-Preserving Page Clustering}
\label{sec:mem_clustering}

To reduce physical memory fragmentation and descriptor overhead, Telix employs a modern page clustering architecture \footcite{chambers03clustering, dickins01clustering}. Unlike early clustering implementations (such as Babao\u{g}lu and Joy's) which violated application binary interfaces by forcing coarse mappings, modern clustering strictly preserves the ABI by distinguishing the allocation unit from the mapping granularity.

Let $K$ (\emph{K\"ornung}, or \texttt{MMUPAGE\_SIZE}) be the ABI-mandated hardware mapping granularity (e.g., 4 KiB).
Let $Z$ (\emph{Zuteilungseinheit}, or \texttt{PAGE\_SIZE}) be the physical allocation unit (e.g., 64 KiB).
Let $B$ (\emph{Bruchst\"uckanzahl}, or \texttt{PAGE\_MMUCOUNT}) be the number of addressable fragments per allocation unit, defined as $B = Z / K$.

When Telix allocates a page, it allocates the full size $Z$. However, virtual memory operations retain the ability to address individual $K$-sized fragments. For instance, if an application requests an \texttt{mmap()} of a 64 KiB file from offset 32 KiB to 36 KiB, the Framekernel sets exactly \emph{one} 4 KiB Page Table Entry (PTE) pointing to that specific fragment within the larger $Z$ allocation. To map the range from 4 KiB to 60 KiB, 14 individual PTEs are set, typically via a highly optimized loop.

This approach reduces the physical page descriptor array footprint by a factor of $B$, entirely transparently to user space. However, it is critical to note that page clustering does not reduce virtual memory overhead; the page table footprint remains unchanged because PTEs still correspond to the fundamental ABI granularity $K$.

\section{Coremaplessness: Lock-Free Physical Memory Management}
\label{sec:mem_coremapless}

While page clustering reduces the required physical descriptor footprint by a factor of $B$, global contiguous descriptor arrays still impose a linear metadata tax. Telix seeks to eliminate this entirely via a **coremapless** architecture.

Telix replaces boot-time-reserved page descriptor arrays with a lock-free adaptation of the \textbf{LLFree} allocator \footcite{litz2021llfree}, modified to utilize strict \textbf{in-band metadata}:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Free Frames:} Metadata (such as next-pointers for lock-free LIFO lists and order-sizes for buddy coalescing) is stored directly inside the physical memory of the unallocated frames themselves.
    \item \textbf{Allocated Frames:} Once a frame is allocated, it is stripped of structural metadata. The burden of tracking the frame (reference counts, reverse mapping) is shifted entirely to the Virtual Memory capability tokens (Morsels) and the software page table extents that actively map it.
\end{itemize}

By pushing metadata exclusively into free space and virtual extents, Telix bounds its physical metadata footprint sublinearly.

\section{Superpaging and the Dense Page Size Spectrum}
\label{sec:mem_superpaging}

While clustering addresses physical fragmentation, **Superpaging** is a hardware-defined acceleration mechanism used to reduce TLB misses and shrink virtual metadata overhead.

Telix bridges page clustering ($Z$) and hardware superpaging seamlessly when executing on architectures that provide a **dense page size spectrum**, such as ARM64's Contiguous Bit or RISC-V's Naturally Aligned Power-Of-Two (NAPOT) extensions.

If an application maps a fully contiguous range of fragments that cover an entire clustered allocation $Z$, and $Z$ aligns with a hardware-supported dense encoding (e.g., RISC-V NAPOT encoding a 64 KiB block into a single base PTE), Telix seamlessly upgrades the mapping into a micro-superpage. If the mapping is subsequently fragmented (e.g., via \texttt{mprotect}), Telix shatters the superpage back into $B$ individual $K$-sized PTEs, ensuring ABI preservation while maximizing opportunistic hardware acceleration.

\section{The VAX-Class Stress Test: MIPS 1 KiB PageGrain}
\label{sec:mem_mips_pagegrain}

To validate the robustness of this ABI-preserving design, Telix targets the MIPS architecture equipped with the \texttt{PageGrain} extension, configured for $K = 1$ KiB hardware pages.

This environment artificially recreates the immense virtual space granularity pressure of legacy VAX systems (which utilized 512 Byte pages). Because modern ABIs universally expect a minimum mapping granularity of 4 KiB, Telix configures its clustering such that $Z \ge 4$ KiB.

The Framekernel transparently manages the bridging of $K = 1$ KiB hardware fragments to fulfill $Z = 4$ KiB ABI expectations. This demonstrates that Telix's hypermodern memory management techniques can gracefully maintain modern ABI compatibility over hostile legacy hardware granularities without structural compromise.

\section{Sublinear Virtual Metadata: Hybrid ART / B+ Trees}
\label{sec:mem_virtual_metadata}

Because page clustering alone does not reduce the number of PTEs required by the hardware, standard multi-level radix page tables suffer from extreme memory bloat during mass-mapping or \texttt{fork} bombs. Telix mitigates this via aggressive range/extent-oriented software structures.

The canonical representation of a Telix address space is not the hardware page table, but a highly compressed software structure combining an \textbf{Adaptive Radix Tree (ART)} and a \textbf{B+ Tree}:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{ART for Dense Extents:} Dense virtual mappings utilize ART structures with adaptive bitslicing and path compression, allowing a massive contiguous shared memory segment to be tracked with a single extent descriptor.
    \item \textbf{B+ Tree for Sparse Ranges:} Highly sparse allocations (such as thread guard pages) are managed via B+ trees to minimize intermediate node allocation.
\end{itemize}

By relying on extent-based sharing, thousands of processes can map the same gigabyte-sized shared library while consuming only a few bytes of virtual metadata per process.

\section{Inverted Page Tables and Software TLB Refills}
\label{sec:mem_ipt}

With virtual memory metadata safely compressed into extent trees, the hardware-defined page tables function merely as a large, fully recoverable translation cache for the hardware MMU.

Telix pursues this logic to its apex by utilizing **Inverted Page Tables (IPT)** where supported. Rather than maintaining a per-process hierarchical page table, an IPT contains one entry per physical frame, indexed by a hash of the Virtual Address and Address Space ID (ASID).

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Software Refill Integration:}] On architectures with software-managed TLBs (e.g., MIPS, early RISC-V profiles), the Telix Framekernel walks the ART/B+ extent trees directly to refill the TLB, bypassing traditional PTE arrays entirely.
    \item[\textbf{Hardware IPT Parallels:}] Telix's software structures heavily mirror historical architectural apexes like the IA-64 Virtual Hash Page Table (VHPT) and IBM POWER's Segment Lookaside Buffers (SLB). Where hardware supports sharing IPT entries across domains, Telix completely eliminates page table duplication for shared memory IPC rings.
\end{description}

\section{Hardware Capability Bounds: CHERI and Morello}
\label{sec:mem_cheri}

As detailed in Chapter~\ref{ch:io}, Telix handles zero-copy memory passing via Capability Morsel Tokens, which encode a base offset and length bound. This software design maps conceptually directly onto hardware-enforced capability architectures, specifically \textbf{CHERI} (Capability Hardware Enhanced RISC Instructions) and the ARM Morello architecture.

Telix is explicitly designed to support CHERI environments. When compiled for Morello, Telix's software Morsel tokens are materialized directly as 128-bit hardware fat pointers. This allows the CPU hardware to physically enforce extent limits during DMA and shared-memory I/O, eliminating the need for the driver domain to perform software-based bounds checking and providing mathematical guarantees against spatial memory safety violations.

\chapter{Memory Architecture: Sublinear Metadata, Granularity, and Design Goals}
\label{ch:memory}

Building upon the initial exploratory prototype developed in the Telix repository \footcite{telix_proto}, this proposal outlines a comprehensive redesign of the Virtual Memory (VM) and physical memory management subsystems. Rather than optimizing for a single metric, the proposed architecture reconciles a broad, disparate set of memory management design goals that frequently contend with one another in traditional operating system kernels.

\section{System Design Goals for Memory Management}
\label{sec:mem_goals}

The proposed Telix memory subsystem is guided by a multi-faceted design matrix intended to solve long-standing bottlenecks in systems software:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Sublinear Management Metadata Footprint:} Achieving true sublinear scaling for internal bookkeeping structures by employing \emph{pervasive extent-based internal accounting} across both physical and virtual layers, eliminating boot-time-reserved static descriptor arrays (such as traditional Linux \texttt{mem\_map}).

    \item \textbf{TLB Reach and Superpaging Performance:} Maximizing execution speed and minimizing hardware Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) misses through opportunistic superpaging, leveraging modern hardware page-size spectra.

    \item \textbf{Cluster-Readiness and Distributed Support:} Accommodating process migration and distributed node topologies without requiring full transparent Network-Oriented Resource Sharing Architecture (NORMA). The VM subsystem must support cluster-wide, cross-node page table invalidation and TLB shootdowns in response to file truncation or distributed page unmapping.

    \item \textbf{Adjusting Fragmentation Frontiers:} Utilizing ABI-preserving page clustering to tune the frontier between internal and external fragmentation, specifically guaranteeing that smaller-size superpage allocations never fail due to external memory fragmentation.

    \item \textbf{Low-Latency Fault Handling and Tail Packing:} Reducing fault latency through incremental faulting strategies for Copy-On-Write (COW) and Zero-Fill-On-Demand (ZFOD) anonymous memory, alongside in-memory tail packing in the page cache for small files to optimize bitblitting and small I/O operations.

    \item \textbf{Hardware Capability Integration:} Mapping software-level memory range tokens directly onto hardware-enforced capability architectures (such as CHERI and ARM Morello) to enforce spatial and temporal memory safety at hardware speeds.
\end{enumerate}

\section{ABI-Preserving Page Clustering and Fragmentation Frontiers}
\label{sec:mem_clustering}

Page clustering is frequently misunderstood as a mechanism for reducing virtual metadata or achieving sublinear scaling. In reality, page clustering achieves only a linear reduction in physical metadata in traditional kernel designs (where global descriptor arrays exist), while leaving virtual page table footprints completely unaltered. In Telix, which eschews global descriptor arrays entirely, page clustering serves a different, highly strategic purpose: **tuning the frontier between internal and external fragmentation**.

\subsection{Nomenclature and Structural Abstraction}

To maintain rigorous compatibility with historical literature and codebases, Telix adopts a unified nomenclature mapping across hardware granularities and historical implementations:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-Reference of Page Clustering Terms and Codebase Macro Equivalents}
\label{tab:clustering_terms}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{l X X X}
\toprule
\textbf{Concept} & \textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{Dickins / Telix Proto} & \textbf{Shutsemau (2026)} \\
\midrule
Mapping Granularity (Hardware) & $K$ (\emph{K\"ornung}) & \texttt{MMUPAGE\_SIZE} & \texttt{PTE\_SIZE} \\
Allocation Unit (Physical) & $Z$ (\emph{Zuteilungseinheit}) & \texttt{PAGE\_SIZE} & \texttt{PG\_SIZE} \\
