Modern page clustering \footcite{chambers03clustering, dickins01clustering, arcangeli07clustering, shutsemau26clustering} strictly preserves application binary interfaces (ABIs) by keeping $K$ as the fundamental mapping granularity.

When Telix allocates memory, physical allocation occurs in units of $Z$. However, virtual memory operations continue to address individual $K$-sized fragments. For example, an \texttt{mmap()} targeting a sub-range within an allocation unit sets only the specific $K$-sized Page Table Entries (PTEs) corresponding to those fragments. Mapping $N$ fragments of size $K$ within a $Z$ allocation is handled via optimized vector loops over the page table.

\subsection{Qualitative Guarantees and Sparse Spectra ($W$)}

Page clustering provides two distinct operational benefits depending on the hardware page-size spectrum:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Sparse Page Size Spectra (Large $W$, \emph{Sprungweite}):}] On architectures with a wide gap between base page sizes and the first non-trivial superpage size (large $W$), page clustering acts as a bridge. However, this is treated as a trivial case: it merely shaves an additive constant off the required order of superpage allocations without changing underlying algorithms.

    \item[\textbf{Dense Spectra and Allocation Guarantees:}] Where page clustering achieves a profound, qualitative breakthrough is for smaller superpage sizes. By maintaining physical allocations at the $Z$ boundary, Telix can **guarantee that allocations for smaller-size superpages will never fail due to external fragmentation**. This eliminates the need for heavyweight, unpredictable runtime memory compaction or reserved pool strategies (such as those historically used in HP-UX).
\end{description}

\section{Pervasive Extent-Based Accounting and Coremaplessness}
\label{sec:mem_coremapless}

Sublinear physical memory management metadata is achieved in Telix not through clustering, but through **Pervasive Extent-Based Internal Accounting**.

Traditional operating systems reserve a contiguous array of physical page descriptors (e.g., Linux \texttt{mem\_map}) at boot time. Telix proposes a completely **coremapless** architecture, replacing static descriptor arrays with a lock-free adaptation of the **LLFree** allocator \footcite{litz2021llfree} utilizing in-band metadata:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Unallocated Memory:} Free physical frames store their own allocation metadata (such as lock-free LIFO list pointers and buddy order markers) directly inside the unallocated memory space itself.
    \item \textbf{Allocated Memory:} Upon allocation, physical frames retain zero structural metadata. Reverse mappings, reference counts, and extent bounds are managed entirely by user-space capability tokens (Morsels) and software virtual memory extent trees.
\end{itemize}

\section{Virtual Metadata, Hardware Walkers, and Inverted Page Tables}
\label{sec:mem_virtual_metadata}

Addressing virtual memory metadata overhead presents a distinct set of trade-offs, dictated heavily by CPU architecture design choices.

\subsection{Mitigating Hardware Page-Table Walkers}

A major challenge in modern CPU design is the presence of mandatory hardware page-table walkers. On architectures that enforce multi-level hierarchical page tables (e.g., x86-64, ARM64, RISC-V), hardware walkers render pure Inverted Page Tables (IPT) unusable for direct hardware MMU fills.

To mitigate virtual metadata bloat under mandatory hardware walkers, Telix proposes **Extent-Driven Page Table Sharing**:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Canonical address space mappings will be maintained in compressed software structures combining **Adaptive Radix Trees (ART)** (for dense extents) and **B+ Trees** (for sparse ranges).
    \item When multiple processes share memory segments (e.g., mass-shared libraries, shared-memory IPC rings, or post-\texttt{fork} execution), the underlying hardware page tables will be shared directly across processes.
    \item To avoid cache-line contention and lock bottlenecks on multi-core systems, shared page table nodes will employ localized replication mechanisms, balancing metadata footprint against concurrent traversal performance.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Inverted Page Tables and Software-Refill Architectures}

On CPU architectures featuring software-managed TLB refills (e.g., MIPS, software-refill RISC-V profiles) or native hardware IPT support (e.g., 64-bit SPARC, IA-64 Virtual Hash Page Tables [VHPT], and IBM POWER Segment Lookaside Buffers [SLB]), Telix will actively utilize Inverted Page Tables.

In these environments, the IPT contains one entry per physical frame, indexed via a hash of the Virtual Address and Address Space ID (ASID). Telix will explore whether hardware-unassisted IPTs in pure software-refill architectures provide a net performance benefit over direct ART/B+ extent tree walks, treating software-refill IPT optimizations as a primary experimental benchmark for the project.

\section{Low-Latency Optimization: Incremental Faulting and Tail Packing}
\label{sec:mem_latency_opt}

To minimize I/O and fault latency, the proposed memory architecture incorporates two key structural mechanisms:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Incremental Anonymous Memory Faulting:}] For Copy-On-Write (COW) and Zero-Fill-On-Demand (ZFOD) anonymous memory, Telix will implement incremental fault handling. Rather than allocating and zeroing full allocation blocks ($Z$) on initial access, the kernel incrementally faults sub-fragments ($K$), reducing initial fault stalls and optimizing bitblitting latency for interactive applications.

    \item[\textbf{In-Memory Tail Packing:}] Small files and trailing file fragments in the page cache frequently waste memory due to internal alignment padding. Telix plans to support in-memory tail packing, packing sub-page file tails into shared physical frames to maximize cache efficiency and reduce storage bus pressure.
\end{description}

\section{Hardware Capability Bounds: CHERI and Morello}
\label{sec:mem_cheri}

The extent-based accounting used throughout the VM subsystem aligns naturally with hardware-enforced capability architectures, specifically **CHERI** and ARM Morello.

When compiled for capability hardware, Telix's software Memory Morsels (base, offset, length) will materialize directly as 128-bit hardware fat pointers. This transfers spatial memory safety enforcement from software checks to the CPU hardware pipeline, providing unforgeable, hardware-bounded memory protection for shared-memory I/O rings and Framekernel capability domains.
